ent at the top-left;\n- pair 4: mirrored left-right, fragment at offset (2, 0);\n- pair 5: rotated 90° (same orientation as pair 3, which is why those two outputs are identical apart from colour), fragment at (12, 10).\n\nFor every pair exactly one (colour, orientation, offset) combination matched the input, and the full picture in that orientation with the input's two colours reproduced the output exactly. So this is a \"completion from a known template\" puzzle: the template is a constant (it is identical across all five pairs, so storing it is legitimate), and the rule is \"find where your fragment sits in the template, then draw the whole template around it\".\n\nTwo things the test inputs force me to handle generally:\n- **Which colour is the drawing?** In test 1 the matched colour is 8, which is the *majority* colour of that grid (7 is the minority). So I cannot take \"background = most common\"; I try each colour of the grid as the foreground and keep the one that matches the template. Test 1 matched only with 8 in orientation rot270 at the top-left; test 2 (colours 5/6) matched with 5 in orientation rot90+flip at offset (4, 10). Each test has a unique match, like the train pairs.\n- **Position.** Test 2 is 11×10, a size never seen in training; the offset search over all placements inside the 20×20 handles this with no size assumptions.\n\nConstruction stages for the student: (1) allocate a 20×20 canvas of the other colour and paste the input fragment at its found offset; (2) complete the rows the fragment spans, by copying the template's cells left and right of it; (3) fill the remaining rows above and below — the finished picture.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    grid = np.asarray(grid)\n    # The fixed 20x20 picture every output shows (1 = drawing colour), read off the train outputs.\n    S = (\"11000000000000011000111111000000000110001111111000000001110011000111000000011100\"\n         \"00000011100000111110000000011100001001100000000011001110011000000000010010100110\"\n         \"00000000011110100110011111111101101001100100000001111010011001111111110010100110\"\n         \"01010000010011100110111011111100001001100101100001000011111011101000010001101100\"\n         \"01011000010001000110111010000100011000110101100010100010111011100001000100111000\")\n    P = np.array([int(c) for c in S]).reshape(20, 20)\n\n    def orient(k, f):\n        R = np.rot90(P, k)\n        return np.fliplr(R) if f else R\n\n    H, W = grid.shape\n    colours, counts = np.unique(grid, return_counts=True)\n    found = None\n    for fg in colours:\n        m = (grid == fg).astype(int)\n        for k in range(4):\n            for f in (False, True):\n                R = orient(k, f)\n                for y in range(21 - H):\n                    for x in range(21 - W):\n                        if np.array_equal(R[y:y+H, x:x+W], m):\n                            found = (fg, k, f, y, x)\n                            break\n                    if found: break\n                if found: break\n            if found: break\n        if found: break\n\n    if found is None:\n        emit(grid, \"No placement of this fragment in the template was found; leaving the input unchanged.\")\n        return grid\n\n    fg, k, f, y0, x0 = found\n    others = [(c, n) for c, n in zip(colours, counts) if c != fg]\n    bg = max(others, key=lambda t: t[1])[0] if others else (0 if fg != 0 else 1)\n    R = orient(k, f)\n    full = np.where(R == 1, fg, bg)\n\n    # Stage 1: allocate the 20x20 canvas and place the given fragment where it belongs in the picture.\n    canvas = np.full((20, 20), bg, dtype=grid.dtype)\n    canvas[y0:y0+H, x0:x0+W] = grid\n    emit(canvas, f\"Allocate a 20x20 canvas of colour {bg}; the input is a fragment of the fixed picture \"\n                 f\"(orientation rot90x{k}{', mirrored' if f else ''}) and sits at rows {y0}-{y0+H-1}, \"\n                 f\"cols {x0}-{x0+W-1}.\")\n\n    # Stage 2: complete the rows the fragment spans using the template.\n    canvas[y0:y0+H, :] = full[y0:y0+H, :]\n    emit(canvas, \"Complete the rows the fragment occupies by copying the template cells to its left and right.\")\n\n    # Stage 3: fill the remaining rows above and below.\n    canvas[:, :] = full\n    emit(canvas, \"Fill the rows above and below the fragment from the template: the whole picture is restored.\")\n    return canvas\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So I cannot take \"background = most common\"; I try each colour of the grid as the foreground and keep the one that matches the template."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"icture, and the whole output *is* that picture — always the same drawing, just rotated/mirrored and recoloured."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"rom one fixed 20×20 picture, and the whole output *is* that picture — always the same drawing, just rotated/mirrored and recoloured."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- pair 2: picture rotated 180°, fragment at offset (0, 6);"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- pair 3: rotated 90°, colours 3/0, fragment at the top-left;"}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So this is a \"completion from a known template\" puzzle: the template is a constant (it is identical across all five pairs, so storing it is legitimate), and the rule is \"find where your fragment sits in the template, then draw the whole template around it\"."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"d = most common\"; I try each colour of the grid as the foreground and keep the one that matches the template."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"te the input fragment at its found offset; (2) complete the rows the fragment spans, by copying the template's cells left and right of it; (3) fill the remaining rows above and below — the finished picture."}],"repair":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Fill the rows above and below the fragment from the template: the whole pictur